{
  "workers": [
    {
      "worker": 1,
      "brief": "You have rows 1‑35 of the ledger. Rows 1‑10 are the rate table (columns: row,rate_code,unit_rate). Rows 11‑35 are line items (columns: row,line_id,claim_id,qty,unit_rate,rate_code,paid). For each line item, determine the applicable unit rate: if the unit_rate field is non‑empty use it, otherwise look up the rate_code in the rate table to get its unit_rate. Compute amount_owed = qty × unit_rate. If paid < amount_owed, record this line as UNDERPAID and note the shortfall = amount_owed – paid (rounded to two decimals). Also track every claim_id you encounter. After processing your rows, output two lists: (1) all underpaid lines you found (include row number, claim_id, shortfall) and (2) any claim_id that appears more than once within the rows you processed (include the row numbers where it appears). Do NOT attempt to resolve duplicates that may appear in the other worker’s range; just report duplicates you can see.",
      "ranges": [[1, 35]]
    },
    {
      "worker": 2,
      "brief": "You have rows 36‑70 of the ledger. Rows 1‑10 (the rate table) are NOT in your range, but you will need the rate codes defined there. Assume the rate table from rows 1‑10 is globally available for lookup. Rows 36‑70 are line items (columns: row,line_id,claim_id,qty,unit_rate,rate_code,paid). For each line item, determine the applicable unit rate: use the unit_rate field if present; otherwise look up the rate_code in the rate table (rows 1‑10). Compute amount_owed = qty × unit_rate. If paid < amount_owed, record this line as UNDERPAID and note the shortfall = amount_owed – paid (rounded to two decimals). Also track every claim_id you encounter. After processing, output two lists: (1) all underpaid lines you found (include row number, claim_id, shortfall) and (2) any claim_id that appears more than once within the rows you processed (include the row numbers where it appears). Do NOT try to resolve duplicates that may exist in the other worker’s range; just report duplicates you can see.",
      "ranges": [[36, 70]]
    }
  ]
}

call.prompt:
You are auditing a remittance ledger with two sections. The rate table lists rate codes and their unit rates. Each line item is one billed service; it gives either its own unit_rate or a rate_code whose unit rate is in the rate table. The amount owed for a line is qty x unit rate. A line is UNDERPAID when paid is less than the amount owed; its shortfall is (amount owed) - paid, in currency units with two decimals. Lines paid exactly, or overpaid, are not underpayments and must not be reported as underpaid. A claim is DUPLICATED when the same claim_id appears on more than one line item (billed more than once). Find every underpaid line and every duplicated claim.

2 workers each checked part of the ledger. You do not see the ledger. Their reports:
Worker 1: {"underpaid": [{"line_id": "RX-X536G", "shortfall": 1140.41}, {"line_id": "RX-9SZ5L", "shortfall": 186.02}, {"line_id": "RX-UXCVS", "shortfall": 115.52}], "duplicates": []}
Worker 2: {"underpaid": [{"line_id": "RX-6ZWMA", "shortfall": 1792.78}, {"line_id": "RX-XHBS6", "shortfall": 1653.14}, {"line_id": "RX-U24KN", "shortfall": 88.01}, {"line_id": "RX-B9WCY", "shortfall": 484.18}, {"line_id": "RX-2W97U", "shortfall": 151.15}], "duplicates": [{"line_ids": ["RX-748CD", "RX-764HZ"]}]}

Produce the final report.
Do not show your working or any explanation. Reply with only a JSON object: {"underpaid": [{"line_id": "<line_id>", "shortfall": <amount with two decimals>}], "duplicates": [{"line_ids": ["<line_id>", "<line_id>"]}]}. List only lines whose shortfall is greater than 0. Use empty lists if there are none.
